<header class="ws-head"><p class="ws-kicker">Worksheet &middot; Reading path</p><h1 class="ws-title">Worksheet: The Fall of Rome</h1><p class="ws-lede">How the western half of the ancient world's greatest state came apart.</p><p class="ws-who" aria-hidden="true"><span>Name</span><span>Class</span><span>Date</span></p><p class="ws-tools"><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="0">Print the worksheet</button><button type="button" class="ws-print" data-key="1">Print with the answers</button><a href="/paths/the-fall-of-rome">Open the path &rarr;</a></p></header>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><p class="ws-how">Number the stops from 1 (earliest) to 6 (latest), by when each began: a state&#8217;s founding, a person&#8217;s birth, the date of an event.</p><ul class="ws-order"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Theodoric the Great</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Byzantine Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Roman Empire</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Odoacer</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Battle of Adrianople</span></li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span><span>Attila</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Match the people to their dates</h2><p class="ws-how">Write the number of the right dates next to each person. Their pages on the path will help.</p><div class="ws-match"><ol class="ws-ma" type="A"><li>Attila</li><li>Odoacer</li><li>Theodoric the Great</li></ol><ol class="ws-mb"><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>454–526</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>c. 433–493 CE</li><li><span class="ws-box" aria-hidden="true"></span>c. 406–453</li></ol></div></section>
<section class="ws-task"><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><ol class="ws-qs"><li><p>Stop 1, <b>Roman Empire</b>. The path begins here because: &#8220;Begin with the whole story: the power the fifth century inherited and squandered.&#8221; What does this stop tell you about how the story began?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 5, <b>Attila</b>: &#8220;The Hun who bled both empires and showed how little stood behind the walls.&#8221; Find one detail on its page that supports this, and one question it leaves open.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>Stop 8, <b>Byzantine Empire</b>: &#8220;The half that did not fall: Rome's eastern empire endured to 1453.&#8221; How does this last stop connect to an earlier one?</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li><li><p>The path sums itself up as: &#8220;How the western half of the ancient world's greatest state came apart.&#8221; Which stop shows this best? Explain your choice.</p><span class="ws-lines" aria-hidden="true"><span></span><span></span><span></span></span></li></ol></section>
<p class="ws-src">From The Epoch Institute: the-epoch-institute.org/paths/the-fall-of-rome. Free to print and copy for use in class.</p>
<details class="ws-key" id="answers"><summary>Answer key</summary><div class="ws-key-b"><p class="ws-key-t">Answer key: Worksheet: The Fall of Rome</p><section><h2><span class="ws-n">1</span>Put the stops in order</h2><ol class="ws-key-list"><li>Roman Empire <span>from 27 BCE</span></li><li>Byzantine Empire <span>from 330 CE</span></li><li>Battle of Adrianople <span>378 CE</span></li><li>Attila <span>born c. 406 CE</span></li><li>Odoacer <span>born c. 433 CE</span></li><li>Theodoric the Great <span>born 454 CE</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">2</span>Match the people to their dates</h2><ol class="ws-key-list" type="A"><li>Attila <span>3: c. 406–453</span></li><li>Odoacer <span>2: c. 433–493 CE</span></li><li>Theodoric the Great <span>1: 454–526</span></li></ol></section><section><h2><span class="ws-n">3</span>Think and discuss</h2><p class="ws-key-note">Open questions: answers will vary. Good answers use details from the pages of the path.</p></section></div></details>
